      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's a decent start, but the dialogue feels a little too philosophical and \"safe.\" it sounds like they're discussing a social studies topic rather than two guys who actually live in this environment and feel the weight of it every day. \n\nin a small Mississippi town in '56, these men wouldn't be talking about \"untangling what we used to be.\" they wouldn't be so contemplative. they'd be more blunt, more annoyed, and more visceral about the fact that these men—who they now see as equals because of their service—are walking around their town like they own the place. can you rewrite this to make it feel more grounded and raw? i want their discomfort to come from a place of genuine resentment and a feeling that the old \"order\" is being ignored. make the tension feel more jagged and less poetic.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
