      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sheriff Samuel, the age-wrinkled face shadowed by decades served, scoffs bitterly. His breath comes out thunderous. \"That bunch with the tan lines and that fancy talk—all wrapped up in their swagger like they got their noses on high steel—ain't got no place strutting around like lords. You think them savvy from those lookouts, now they're playing our fools—watching us here to tip our hats.\"\n\nJimmy's low growl scratches the table's surface, his gaze hard as broken glass, defiant as rusty hinge. He lights a cigarette, watching it clash with his thoughts, the smoky ring hiss as a ghost of defiance. \"Maybe we're wired up fine since we bled for our man. But that damn vote – it's a mite of goddamn change, ain't it? Now these boys from the war, just swaggered through hell and back, and look at 'em, acting like they got their holy nose up our horn. Tell your rich uncle's son all the nice things he's done.\"\n\nThomas, Sam's younger brother, sits hunched at the dim light, chewing on his molar and deflecting more than just smoke. \"Yeah, well, we all loved",
